
---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="eyebrow">BANK OF MINCE</p>
    <h1>About the Bank</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The Bank of Mince is central monetary authority responsible for monetary policy,
      treasury management, economic stability and the development of
      the server economy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="about-the-bank">
    <h2>About the Bank of Mince</h2>
    <p>
      The Bank of Mince serves as the central monetary authority of
      the server economy. Its responsibilities are to manage state
      reserves, administer taxation, issue financial instruments,
      monitor economic conditions and publish official economic
      statistics and reports.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Bank aims to maintain a stable and functional economy,
      preserve the value of the currency, encourage the exchange of
      goods and services, and support sustainable economic development.
      Its powers, responsibilities and governance are established by
      the <a href="/charter/">Charter of the Bank of Mince</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      The Bank publishes economic reports, monetary policy announcements,
      meeting minutes and research papers to promote transparency and
      provide players with information about the state of the economy.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="contact">
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Questions, proposals and concerns regarding monetary policy,
      taxation, financial services or the operation of the Bank may
      be raised with the Governor or at the relevant discussions.
    </p>
    <p>
      Players may also raise policy proposals and motions through the
      public oversight mechanisms established in the <a href="/charter/#section-vii">Charter's governance provisions</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section" id="glossary">
    <p class="eyebrow">REFERENCE</p>
    <h2>Glossary of Economic Terms</h2>
    <p class="muted">
      Definitions of terms used in the Bank's publications, monetary
      policy announcements, financial services and economic reports.
    </p>

    <nav class="glossary-index card" aria-label="Glossary contents">
      <h3>Contents</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#currency">Currency and Money</a></li>
        <li><a href="#economic-indicators">Economic Indicators</a></li>
        <li><a href="#taxation">Taxation and Production</a></li>
        <li><a href="#banking">Banking and the Treasury</a></li>
        <li><a href="#bonds">Bonds and Debt</a></li>
        <li><a href="#loans">Loans and Lending</a></li>
        <li><a href="#policy">Monetary Policy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#governance">Governance and Publications</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="currency">
      <h3>Currency and Money</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-currency">
          <dt>Currency</dt>
          <dd>
            A medium of exchange used to purchase goods and services,
            settle debts and transfer wealth between players. Raw Beef
            is the official state currency under the current Charter.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-circulation">
          <dt>Currency in Circulation</dt>
          <dd>
            The total amount of currency currently held or used by
            players and businesses outside the Bank's own reserves.
            It measures the money available for transactions in the
            wider economy.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-money-supply">
          <dt>Money Supply</dt>
          <dd>
            The total amount of money included within a defined measure
            of the economy.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-liquidity">
          <dt>Liquidity</dt>
          <dd>
            The ease with which an asset can be used or converted into
            currency for spending. Currency is highly liquid, while
            assets that are difficult to sell or exchange are less liquid.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-reserves">
          <dt>Reserves</dt>
          <dd>
            Currency and other assets held by the Bank to support its
            operations, meet obligations and respond to economic
            disturbances. Reserves may include currency, financial
            claims and strategic assets.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-full-reserve-backing">
          <dt>Full Reserve Backing</dt>
          <dd>
            The principle that the Bank's relevant monetary liabilities
            are backed by sufficient declared reserves.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="economic-indicators">
      <h3>Economic Indicators</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-inflation">
          <dt>Inflation</dt>
          <dd>
            An increase in the general price level of goods and services
            over time. When prices rise, a unit of currency buys less
            than it did previously.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-deflation">
          <dt>Deflation</dt>
          <dd>
            A sustained decrease in the general price level. Deflation
            increases the purchasing power of currency, although it
            can also discourage spending if players expect prices to
            continue falling.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-purchasing-power">
          <dt>Purchasing Power</dt>
          <dd>
            The quantity of goods and services that a unit of currency
            can buy. Purchasing power falls when prices rise relative
            to the amount of currency held.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-wealth">
          <dt>Wealth</dt>
          <dd>
            The value of the assets held by a player or organisation,
            minus any liabilities they owe. Wealth differs from income,
            which measures the flow of earnings received over a period.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-inequality">
          <dt>Economic Inequality</dt>
          <dd>
            Differences in the distribution of income, currency holdings
            or total wealth between players. Inequality may be measured
            using wealth shares, income distributions or statistical
            measures such as the Gini coefficient.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-gini">
          <dt>Gini Coefficient</dt>
          <dd>
            A measure of inequality ranging from 0 to 1, where 0
            represents perfect equality and 1 represents maximum
            inequality.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-velocity">
          <dt>Velocity of Money</dt>
          <dd>
            A measure of how frequently currency changes hands during
            a given period.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="taxation">
      <h3>Taxation and Production</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-tax">
          <dt>Tax</dt>
          <dd>
            A compulsory payment to the state, collected according to
            the applicable rules. Taxes may be levied on production,
            income, wealth, financial returns or other specified activities.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-progressive-tax">
          <dt>Progressive Taxation</dt>
          <dd>
            A tax structure in which higher levels of the taxable
            activity are subject to higher marginal tax rates. This
            reduces the tax burden on smaller producers while
            collecting a greater share from larger producers.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-tax-free-allowance">
          <dt>Tax-Free Allowance</dt>
          <dd>
            The amount of eligible production or income that can be
            generated without incurring the relevant tax during a
            specified period.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-marginal-tax-rate">
          <dt>Marginal Tax Rate</dt>
          <dd>
            The tax rate applied to the next unit of taxable production
            or income. A 100% marginal tax rate means that each
            additional taxable unit is matched by an equal tax
            liability under the relevant rules; it does not necessarily
            mean that all earlier production is taxed at 100%.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-production-limit">
          <dt>Production Limit</dt>
          <dd>
            A threshold used to regulate the amount of currency-generating
            production. Under the Bank's taxation framework, production
            above a specified soft limit may face a higher marginal tax
            rate, potentially reaching 100%.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-tax-revenue">
          <dt>Tax Revenue</dt>
          <dd>
            Currency or other eligible assets collected by the state
            through taxation during a given period. Tax revenue can
            fund public expenditure, build reserves and support
            economic stabilisation.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-tax-base">
          <dt>Tax Base</dt>
          <dd>
            The activity, income or quantity against which a tax is
            assessed. For example, a meat production tax may use the
            quantity of eligible Raw Beef produced during a week.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="banking">
      <h3>Banking and the Treasury</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-central-bank">
          <dt>Central Bank</dt>
          <dd>
            An institution responsible for monetary policy and the
            management of currency-related operations. The Bank of
            Mince fulfils this role within the server economy.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-treasury">
          <dt>Treasury</dt>
          <dd>
            The collection of currency, financial claims and other
            assets held by the state or Bank. Treasury resources may
            be used for expenditure, lending, debt repayment,
            stabilisation and strategic investment.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-balance-sheet">
          <dt>Balance Sheet</dt>
          <dd>
            A financial statement recording an institution's assets and
            liabilities at a particular point in time.
            Assets are what the Bank owns or is owed; liabilities are
            what the Bank owes to others.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-assets">
          <dt>Assets</dt>
          <dd>
            Resources or claims with economic value owned or controlled
            by a player or organisation. For the Bank, these may include
            currency reserves, loans receivable, bonds held and other
            strategic holdings.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-liabilities">
          <dt>Liabilities</dt>
          <dd>
            Obligations owed to other parties. A Bank liability may
            include deposits owed to account holders or outstanding
            financial obligations under bonds it has issued.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-deposit">
          <dt>Deposit</dt>
          <dd>
            Currency placed with the Bank and recorded as belonging
            to an account holder. Depending on the account terms,
            a deposit may earn interest or be used to facilitate
            transfers and scheduled payments.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-public-expenditure">
          <dt>Public Expenditure</dt>
          <dd>
            Spending by the state or Bank on public projects,
            government organisations, economic interventions,
            services or other authorised purposes.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-surplus">
          <dt>Budget Surplus</dt>
          <dd>
            A situation in which the state's income exceeds its
            expenditure over a defined period. A deficit occurs
            when expenditure exceeds income.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="bonds">
      <h3>Bonds and Debt</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-bond">
          <dt>Bond</dt>
          <dd>
            A debt instrument through which an issuer borrows currency
            from an investor in exchange for a promise to make payments
            under specified terms. A bond normally states its principal,
            interest arrangements and maturity date.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-government-bond">
          <dt>Government Bond</dt>
          <dd>
            A bond issued by the state or Central Bank to borrow funds.
            Government bonds may be used to finance expenditure,
            attract savings or remove currency from circulation.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-principal">
          <dt>Principal</dt>
          <dd>
            The original amount borrowed or invested, excluding
            interest. For a bond, the principal is normally the amount
            repaid at maturity.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-interest">
          <dt>Interest</dt>
          <dd>
            The amount paid for the use of borrowed currency or earned
            on an eligible financial asset. Interest is calculated
            according to the applicable rate, principal and period.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-interest-rate">
          <dt>Interest Rate</dt>
          <dd>
            The rate used to calculate interest on a loan, bond,
            deposit or other financial instrument. Rates should always
            be interpreted alongside their time period and payment
            arrangements.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-coupon">
          <dt>Coupon</dt>
          <dd>
            A scheduled interest payment made to the holder of a
            coupon bond. The coupon amount is determined by the
            bond's principal, coupon rate and payment schedule.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-coupon-bond">
          <dt>Coupon Bond</dt>
          <dd>
            A bond that pays interest periodically throughout its
            term, with the principal repaid at maturity. For example,
            a bond may pay a fixed amount of interest every week
            until the original principal is returned.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-zero-coupon-bond">
          <dt>Zero-Coupon Bond</dt>
          <dd>
            A bond that makes no periodic coupon payments. Instead,
            the principal and accumulated interest are paid together
            at maturity, according to the bond's terms.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-maturity">
          <dt>Maturity</dt>
          <dd>
            The date on which a financial instrument reaches the end
            of its term. For a bond, maturity is normally when the
            outstanding principal becomes due for repayment.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-yield">
          <dt>Yield</dt>
          <dd>
            A measure of the return earned on a financial instrument
            relative to its price or amount invested.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-outstanding-debt">
          <dt>Outstanding Debt</dt>
          <dd>
            The principal amount of debt that has been issued but
            not yet repaid. It may refer to the Bank's liabilities
            from issued bonds or to the debts owed by borrowers.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="loans">
      <h3>Loans and Lending</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-loan">
          <dt>Loan</dt>
          <dd>
            An agreement under which the Bank provides currency to
            a borrower, who agrees to repay the amount borrowed
            according to specified terms. A loan may require interest
            payments, principal repayments or both.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-borrower">
          <dt>Borrower</dt>
          <dd>
            The person, business or organisation that receives a loan
            and is responsible for meeting its repayment obligations.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-lender">
          <dt>Lender</dt>
          <dd>
            The person or institution providing currency under a
            loan agreement. In a Bank-issued loan, the Bank is
            the lender and the recipient is the borrower.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-loan-term">
          <dt>Loan Term</dt>
          <dd>
            The period during which a loan remains in effect,
            including any scheduled repayment period. The term
            determines when obligations must be fulfilled under
            the agreement.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-collateral">
          <dt>Collateral</dt>
          <dd>
            An asset pledged as security for a loan. If the borrower
            fails to meet the agreement's conditions, the lender may
            have rights over the collateral as specified in the
            applicable rules.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-secured-loan">
          <dt>Secured Loan</dt>
          <dd>
            A loan backed by collateral. The collateral provides
            a potential means of recovering value if the borrower
            defaults, subject to the loan agreement.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-limited-liability">
          <dt>Limited Liability Loan</dt>
          <dd>
            A loan whose repayment obligations are limited to the
            borrowing organisation and the assets specified under
            the agreement. Under the Bank's Charter, eligible business
            and government loans do not create personal liability
            for the owners or individuals associated with those
            organisations.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-default">
          <dt>Default</dt>
          <dd>
            A failure to meet an obligation under a financial
            agreement, such as missing a required repayment.
            The consequences depend on the terms of the loan or bond.
          </dd>
        </div>

      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="policy">
      <h3>Monetary Policy</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-monetary-policy">
          <dt>Monetary Policy</dt>
          <dd>
            The policies and actions used by the Bank to influence
            monetary conditions, currency supply, borrowing, saving
            and economic activity. Instruments may include interest
            rates, taxation and other measures authorised by the Charter.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-base-rate">
          <dt>Base Interest Rate</dt>
          <dd>
            A reference interest rate used by the Bank when setting
            or assessing other rates, including certain loan rates.
            Its precise role and value are determined by the Bank's
            current policy.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-economic-stabilisation">
          <dt>Economic Stabilisation</dt>
          <dd>
            Actions intended to reduce harmful economic fluctuations,
            address excessive inflation or deflation, and maintain
            a functioning market for goods and services.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-emergency-measures">
          <dt>Emergency Monetary Measures</dt>
          <dd>
            Temporary interventions authorised under the Charter
            during severe inflation, economic collapse or monetary
            instability. These measures must be proportionate to
            the threat they are intended to address.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>

    <div class="glossary-group" id="governance">
      <h3>Governance and Publications</h3>

      <dl class="glossary-list">
        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-governor">
          <dt>Governor</dt>
          <dd>
            The person responsible for overseeing the Bank's daily
            operations and implementing policy in accordance with
            the Charter and the current public mandate.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-public-mandate">
          <dt>Public Mandate</dt>
          <dd>
            The broad economic objectives and priorities established
            through public discussion, summit agreements and other
            recognised governance mechanisms.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-economic-summit">
          <dt>Economic Summit</dt>
          <dd>
            A meeting open to players for discussing long-term
            economic goals, reviewing the state of the economy
            and establishing or revising the public mandate.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-audit">
          <dt>Audit</dt>
          <dd>
            An inspection of financial records, currency holdings,
            production or relevant activities to verify information,
            support taxation and investigate suspected fraud or
            undeclared reserves.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-economic-report">
          <dt>Economic Report</dt>
          <dd>
            The Bank's regular publication describing economic
            conditions, monetary developments, treasury affairs,
            financial obligations and other relevant statistics
            for a defined reporting period.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-meeting-minutes">
          <dt>Meeting Minutes</dt>
          <dd>
            The formal written record of a meeting, documenting
            relevant discussions, decisions, motions and agreed
            actions. Minutes provide an official reference for
            future policy and governance decisions.
          </dd>
        </div>

        <div class="glossary-entry" id="term-research-paper">
          <dt>Research Paper</dt>
          <dd>
            A publication examining a particular economic question,
            policy proposal or financial mechanism in greater depth.
            Research papers may inform future decisions but do not
            automatically constitute adopted Bank policy.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
